v2.2.9  (work in progress)
      * New viewer: mcpltool -w FILE (or mcpl.view(FILE) in Python) shows
        overviews and plots of the particles, in a window or in the
        terminal, where the particles can be picked and edited, and written
        to a new file. The plots are in groups for some uses (e.g. -wbeamz
        for a beam along z), each with an overview. In a window, an align
        helper adds edits turning the mean direction of the particles into a
        given direction, or moving their mean position to a point. New extra:
        "curses".
      * Python fixes: MCPLFile closes the file of gzipped files, dump_file
        closes the file it opened, a plain 0 in hypot, min, max and clamp has
        the units of the other values, histogram() checks its bins and range,
        text plots have no round-off values in log scales, and matplotlib
        needs a display to show plots also outside Linux. Statistics with
        picks no longer fail for blocks without picked particles, swap(..)
        refuses wavelength, and values of display_by are only labelled as
        conditions if all are 0 or 1.
      * Files are compressed with gzip level 1 (instead of 6 in C and 9 in
        Python), which for MCPL files is several times faster and makes files
        only slightly larger. Files compressed at any level can be read.
      * Expressions can use the fields of the userdata, as userdata.NAME (and
        userdata.NAME.at(INDEX) for arrays), with the units of their layout
        (and the new unit K), e.g. -p "userdata.temp > 300K". Integers work
        essentially as in C, but with errors for overflow and for unsigned
        integers combined with negative values. The operator / always divides
        as floating point numbers (1/2 is 0.5), and // divides non-negative
        integers (7//2 is 3). The operator ~ is no longer supported, and & and
        | only work on integers.
      * Some more names are reserved for userdata fields (e.g. class, from,
        if and pass).
      * mcpl_closeandgzip_outfile no longer compresses output files left
        unfinished by earlier errors (so they can still be repaired), and
        returns 0 for them.
      * mcpltool --extract keeps the uncompressed output (with a warning) if
        compressing it fails, as --merge does, and output names of existing
        directories are refused.
      * mcpl_name_helper no longer crashes for names ending with a directory
        separator (with mode "b", like mcpl.name_helper in Python, which gave
        another result).
      * mcpl_transfer_last_read_particle gives an error unless a particle
        was just read from the source file (it transferred the previous
        particle again after the end of the file, a rewind or a dump).
      * Uncompressed files whose size does not agree with the number of
        particles in their header (e.g. truncated files) can no longer be
        opened (in C and Python), as the format requires. Files with 0
        particles (not closed properly) are still recovered when opened, and
        truncated files can be repaired with mcpltool --repair.
      * The file position (current_pos) of gzipped files read with
        mcpl_generic_fread was wrong after reading more than 32 kB at once,
        which could give wrong output from mcpl2ssw with gzipped reference
        files.
      * mcpl_merge_files and mcpl_forcemerge_files no longer overwrite an
        input file when ".mcpl" is appended to the name of the output file
        (e.g. output "a" with input "a.mcpl").
      * stat:sum values, and numbers in the files of mcpltool --text, are
        always written and read with "." as the decimal point, also in
        applications using a locale with another one.
      * Plots in the terminal (the unicode and ascii plotting backends) are
        shown in colour: curves with the colours of matplotlib (several curves
        drawn as lines of braille dots with the unicode backend), and 2D maps
        with the viridis colour map (with the unicode backend, drawn with half
        blocks, so with twice as many rows). Colours are used when the output
        is a terminal, unless the NO_COLOR environment variable is set, and
        always if FORCE_COLOR is set. The new mcpltool option
        --color=auto|always|never (and the unicode_color and ascii_color
        options of the backends) overrides this. Files are always written
        without colours.
      * New usage information for mcpltool: -h shows a single page with the
        common options, --full-help shows all options, and -p help, -E help
        and -H help describe how to write the expressions, with all particle
        fields, units, functions and histogram options.
      * Expressions (with -p, -E and -H, and in the Python API) use the
        logical operators and, or and not (&&, || and ! give an error saying
        what to write instead), and mod(a,b) for remainders of integers. The
        position of the particles in the file can be used as fileidx (e.g.
        mcpltool -e -p "mod(fileidx,10) == 0" FILE OUT.mcpl.gz extracts every
        tenth particle, marking stat:sum entries as not available). They
        can only contain printable ASCII characters, and not the characters
        " ' { } [ ] # $ ` and backslash.
      * MCPLFile has the new options skip and limit (like mcpltool -s and -l),
        which choose a range of the particles in the file. All arguments of
        MCPLFile after the file name must now be given by name (e.g.
        blocklength=1000).
      * New option mcpltool --create-examples[=N], which creates three example
        files with N particles each (default 1000000) in the current
        directory: neutrons leaving a moderator surface, mixed particles
        around a target, and a polarised neutron beam with userdata. The
        particles are generated from simple distributions, for trying out
        mcpltool and the mcpl Python package with large files.
      * The minimum CMake version is now 3.21 (and on Windows 3.27, which was
        already needed there, now with a clear error message).
      * The command-line tools are renamed: the Python tool from the
        mcpl-python package (pymcpltool) is now mcpltool, and the compiled
        tool from the mcpl-core package (mcpltool) is now mcpltool-c. The
        pymcpltool command still works, but warns that it has been renamed.
        In Python, app_mcpltool is the new name of app_pymcpltool (which also
        still works, but warns). The usage information shown with "python -m
        mcpl" shows "python -m mcpl" as the name of the command.
      * The MCPL_BINDIR, MCPL_LIBDIR, MCPL_INCDIR, MCPL_LIBNAME,
        MCPL_CMD_MCPLTOOL and MCPL_CMD_MCPLCONFIG variables provided by
        find_package(MCPL) in CMake had wrong (or empty) values. The
        MCPL_CMD_MCPLTOOL variable is now the path of mcpltool-c.
      * mcpltool and pymcpltool no longer leave output files behind when
        --merge, --forcemerge or --extract fail (mcpltool left an empty file,
        and pymcpltool --extract a file with an incomplete header).
      * ssw2mcpl and mcpl2ssw read and write the numbers of histories (np1),
        particles (nrss) and histories with particles (niss) in the headers
        of MCNP5 and MCNP6 SSW files as the 64 bit integers they are (issue
        #207). Previously only the lower 32 bits were used for MCNP6 files,
        which for files with more than 2147483647 histories gave a wrong
        number of histories and could make the header parsing fail (the
        actual cause of the "unexpected record" in issue #45), and MCNP5 files
        with more than 2147483647 histories were rejected (discussion #118).
        Files with fewer histories were always handled correctly.
      * mcpl2ssw now sets niss in the header of the new SSW file to the number
        of histories with particles (which is the number of particles, since
        each particle is given its own history), instead of copying it from
        the reference SSW file (issue #207).
      * mcpl2ssw no longer counts particles which it skips (because their
        PDG codes have no MCNP equivalent) in the history numbers of the
        particles, which could then exceed the number of histories (np1) in
        the header of the new SSW file.
      * The documentation states that blobs are stored byte for byte, so the
        byte order of binary numbers in them is chosen by the application.
      * The C and Python readers refuse files whose particle size in the header
        does not match the fields of the particles (it was only checked for
        files with userdata, and a size of 0 could lead to a division by
        zero), and files with a universal weight which is not positive and
        finite (as required when writing; the C reader could otherwise read
        beyond the particle data, when the universal weight was 0).
      * Blob keys must be unique, non-empty, ASCII, with no non-printable
        characters, whitespace, backslash, or quotation marks.
        This is required for files in MCPL format version 4 (which are
        refused otherwise), and the C, C++ and Python APIs refuse other keys
        when they are added to new files. Keys copied from the headers of
        files in older format versions (e.g. when merging files) are still
        accepted, unless the new file has userdata (and is thus in format
        version 4).
      * The flags in the header (userflags, polarisation, single precision
        and universal weight) must have exactly the values of the format (0
        or 1, and in version 4 the userflags field can also have 0x2), and
        files with other values are refused by the C and Python readers, as
        are files with null characters in strings (now also refused by the
        Python reader and writer).
      * Fields of userdata layouts can have units, as in "temp:float32:K" or
        "q:float64:cm^-1.MeV": cm, MeV, ms, K and deg, in this order, each at
        most once, separated by dots, with powers like cm^2 or MeV^-1. Other
        units make the layout invalid. mcpl-python gives the units to scipp
        (MCPLFile.to_scipp), and C++ fields get them with
        add_userdata_field<float>("temp","K").
      * Userdata fields can not be named like the fields of particles (x,
        ekin, userflags, ...), or position, direction, polarisation or
        wavelength.
      * Userdata is optional in MCPL format version 4, signalled by 0x2 in the
        userflags field of the header (which also holds 0x1 if userflags are
        enabled). Only with userdata does the header contain the userdata
        layout. Files in version 4 without userdata are thus identical to
        files in version 3 except for the version number, so any such file
        can be turned into the other by changing the version number (for
        version 3 files without NaN or infinite values). MCPL itself still
        writes files without userdata in version 3.
      * mcpl-python: blocks without particles now always behave like other
        blocks, with all fields being arrays of length 0. Previously, selecting
        or slicing a block which was already empty (e.g. block[mask][:] with
        an empty selection) gave a block whose fields could not be accessed.
      * mcpl-python: new methods MCPLFile.to_scipp and MCPLFile.scipp_blocks
        (one DataArray per block), giving the particles as a scipp DataArray
        with the weights as data (with variances) and the particle fields as
        coordinates with units (x, y, z [cm], ekin [MeV], time [ms], ...),
        plus polarisation, userflags and userdata fields where present.
        Positions, directions and polarisations can also be given as
        3-vectors, and extra coordinates can be calculated with expressions
        (with units following from the expressions). For files, skip, limit
        and pick parameters choose the particles, and by default at most
        100 million particles are converted (with a warning when particles
        are left out). This needs scipp 26.9 or later, which can be installed
        with the new "scipp" extra (pip install "mcpl-python[scipp]"), which
        is not part of the "all" extra since scipp needs Python 3.12 or later.
      * All Python dependencies of the MCPL packages, and of the development
        and testing of MCPL, are now defined in devel/dependency_database.yml
        (as in NCrystal). The developer tool checks that the pyproject.toml
        files agree with it, and generates the requirements and conda
        environment files in devel/reqs/ ("mcpldevtool depdb --mode
        fixreqfiles"). Dependencies which can not be installed everywhere
        have conditions there (needed Python version, and systems where they
        are not available), which become environment markers in the
        requirements, and tests needing them are not added at all where they
        can not be installed. The CI with the minimum versions of the
        dependencies and the FreeBSD CI take their packages from it too. The
        mcpl package has a new "devel" extra with the development
        dependencies.
      * The C++ interface (mcplcpp.h) is documented with Doxygen comments,
        from which the developer tool command "mcpldevtool cppapi" generates
        the reference documentation (as Markdown, for the website).
      * New header-only C++ interface, mcplcpp.h (requires C++17 or later),
        built on the _es functions of the C API (issue #61). Errors are
        reported as exceptions (mcpl::Error, mcpl::IOError and
        std::bad_alloc). Files are read with mcpl::MCPLFile (with range-based
        for loops over the particles, which are lightweight mcpl::Particle
        views) and written with mcpl::MCPLOutFile (which leaves files
        unfinished unless they are explicitly closed). Userdata fields are
        accessed through typed field objects (e.g.
        userdata_field<float,3>("dir")), and output files can get their
        userdata layout one field at a time. All other functions of the C API
        have counterparts, including the operations on files, and
        set_print_handler accepts any std::function. An example is in
        examples/example_cppmcpl.cc.
      * New C function mcpl_dump_file_es, which dumps the header and particles
        of a file which is already open (like mcpl_dump), with the output
        passed to a function given by the caller (or the print handler).
      * New C function mcpl_check_error, which passes an error in an error
        state to the error handler (like the functions without _es would).
        This allows code relying on the error handler to use the functions
        which are only available with _es.
      * New C function mcpl_close_outfile_unfinished_es, which closes an
        output file without finishing it (as needed when abandoning files,
        e.g. after exceptions in C++), and mcpl_outfile_userdata_layout, which
        gives the userdata layout of an output file.
      * Error-state (_es) variants of the C functions for operations on
        files: mcpl_merge_files_es, mcpl_can_merge_es, mcpl_merge_inplace_es,
        mcpl_forcemerge_files_es, mcpl_repair_es and mcpl_gzip_file_es.
        Errors found before any output is written leave all files unchanged.
        Errors while merging leave the output file (for mcpl_merge_inplace_es
        the first file) unfinished, so it can be recovered with mcpl_repair.
        Errors while compressing remove the partially written .gz file and
        keep the original file. With these, all C functions which can raise
        errors have _es variants.
      * mcpl_gzip_file (and mcpl_gzip_file_es) remove the partially written
        .gz file after errors, instead of leaving it behind.
      * mcpl_merge_inplace now checks for errors when flushing and closing
        the updated file.
      * Error-state (_es) variants of the C functions for writing files:
        mcpl_create_outfile_es, mcpl_hdr_set_srcname_es,
        mcpl_hdr_add_comment_es, mcpl_hdr_add_data_es, the mcpl_enable_*_es
        functions, mcpl_hdr_add_stat_sum_es, mcpl_hdr_scale_stat_sums_es,
        mcpl_get_empty_particle_es, mcpl_add_particle_es,
        mcpl_add_particle_with_userdata_es, mcpl_close_outfile_es,
        mcpl_closeandgzip_outfile_es, mcpl_transfer_metadata_es and
        mcpl_transfer_last_read_particle_es. Invalid arguments leave files
        unchanged, while errors when writing data leave output files
        unusable: they can then only be closed, which leaves the file
        unfinished (so it can often be recovered with mcpl_repair).
      * Write errors which are only detected when buffered data is actually
        written (e.g. with full disks) are now reported when writing the
        header and when closing files, instead of silently producing broken
        files. When closing, all data is written before the number of
        particles is updated in the header, so files with such errors look
        unfinished and can be recovered.
      * Headers are limited to 1.9 GB and 1000000 comments and blobs
        (together), when writing and reading files, in C and Python (as the
        MCPL format allows).
      * Error messages of mcpl_transfer_last_read_particle about universal
        pdgcodes and weights no longer end with a newline.
      * New variants of the C functions for reading files, which report
        errors to the caller instead of ending the program: mcpl_open_file_es,
        mcpl_read_es, mcpl_read_with_userdata_es, mcpl_skipforward_es,
        mcpl_rewind_es, mcpl_seek_es, mcpl_hdr_comment_es,
        mcpl_hdr_stat_sum_es, mcpl_userdata_layout_size_es and
        mcpl_userdata_offset_es. They take a pointer to an mcpl_errstate_t,
        whose code is set by each call (0, or MCPL_ERROR_RUNTIME,
        MCPL_ERROR_IO or MCPL_ERROR_MEMORY in case of errors, along with a
        message). After an error while reading particles, files can only be
        closed. The functions
        without _es are unchanged (errors still go to the error handler).
      * New function mcpl_set_print_handler_ctx, like mcpl_set_print_handler
        but with a context pointer passed to the handler.
      * mcpl_close_file does nothing when called with a file object which
        failed to open (internal NULL), and the reading functions give an
        error for such objects instead of an assertion failure.
      * Opening corrupted files with invalid numbers of comments or blobs, or
        invalid lengths of strings or blobs, in their headers no longer
        allocates memory according to these numbers (which could exhaust the
        memory), but only as the data is actually read (with the buffers
        growing geometrically, to keep this fast). Too large headers, and too
        many comments and blobs, are refused before reading them (e.g. for
        small gzipped files with huge headers).
      * New pymcpltool option --dry-run (-d), which does everything except
        writing files: the same checks, warnings, errors and messages as the
        real run (with "Would ..." instead of "Successfully ..."), followed
        by a note that nothing was written. It applies to --merge,
        --forcemerge, --extract, --repair and --text, and is ignored with
        commands which do not write files (so it can always be added to be
        sure that nothing is written). The Python API gets a corresponding
        dry_run argument for merge_files, forcemerge_files, merge_inplace and
        repair.
      * Errors about files which can not be merged now say why, i.e. the first
        difference found in the headers (e.g. "FP precision: single vs
        double", "universal pdgcode: 2112 vs none", "comment 1 differs"), in
        mcpl_merge_files, mcpl_merge_inplace, mcpltool and the Python API.
      * Forced merges (mcpl_forcemerge_files, mcpltool --forcemerge and the
        Python API) now report the settings which change relative to the
        input files (e.g. "Files have different FP precision (single and
        double), using double") and the data which is discarded (userflags
        unless kept, comments and blobs).
      * Optional dependencies (extras) for the plotting backends of the Python
        API: pip install "mcpl[plot]" (or "mcpl-python[plot]") also installs
        matplotlib (>=3.6), as does "[all]".
      * The minimum versions of the Python dependencies (numpy 1.22, and the
        matplotlib 3.6 of the extras) are now tested in CI.
      * The fields x, y, z and time of particles and blocks of particles read
        with the Python API are now always double precision numbers (like in
        the C API), also for files which store numbers in single precision
        (before, they were single precision numpy values for such files).
      * Expressions have vectors (pos, dir, pol, and (a,b,c)) with the
        functions dot, cross and angle (precise also for small angles, e.g.
        "angle(dir,(0,0,1)) < 1deg"), round, through_sphere(center,radius),
        in and not in, and variables before the condition of picks. Edits
        have rotate_around and rotate_frame. Errors say where in the
        expression the problem is. Numbers are decimal or hexadecimal (like
        0x1f), as in C.
      * New plotting backends "unicode" and "ascii" in the Python API and
        mcpltool, which draw plots as text: in the terminal (e.g. with
        mcpltool -H ... -t), or in .txt files. They need
        no extra Python packages, so plots can always be made. Histograms
        with more bins than the columns (or cells) of such plots are shown
        with the average of the bin values over each column, and histograms
        from mcpltool -H without bins=N are made at the resolution of
        the plot, with a whole number of columns per bin (the number of
        columns is given by mcpl.plotting.resolution(..)).
      * Histogram strings (of mcpltool -H and mcpl.histogram) with three
        expressions, like "x : y : z", give 3D plots of a sample of the
        particles (in windows).
      * Unless the number of bins is given, mcpl.histogram, mcpl.histogram2d
        and mcpltool -H now choose it from the effective number of
        entries N: sqrt(N) bins (the cube root per axis for 2D histograms),
        between 10 and 400, rather than always 100.
      * Particles can now carry custom per-particle data ("userdata"): a fixed
        number of bytes per particle, described by a layout string stored in
        the file header (like "temp:float32,origin:int32,tags:uint8[3]", or
        "data:uint8[27]" for plain bytes). New C functions mcpl_enable_userdata,
        mcpl_add_particle_with_userdata, mcpl_read_with_userdata,
        mcpl_last_read_userdata, mcpl_hdr_userdata_layout and
        mcpl_hdr_userdata_size, as well as helper functions for encoding and
        decoding the fields (mcpl_userdata_offset, mcpl_userdata_get_float32,
        mcpl_userdata_set_float32, etc.). The mcpl_particle_t struct and all
        existing functions are unchanged. Files with userdata are written in
        the new MCPL format version 4, while all other files are still written
        in format version 3 (so they can still be read by older MCPL
        releases). MCPL_FORMATVERSION is now 4, the latest supported format
        version. In files with userdata, particles must be added with
        mcpl_add_particle_with_userdata (with NULL for userdata with all bytes
        zero), and mcpl_add_particle is an error, as is transferring particles
        without userdata into such files. Files can only be merged if their
        userdata layouts are identical, while forced merges discard the
        userdata (with a warning) unless all files have the same layout.
      * The Python API can read files with userdata: MCPLFile.userdata_layout
        and userdata_size, and block.userdata (and particle.userdata), which
        is a numpy structured array with the fields of the layout (e.g.
        block.userdata['temp']). The userdata is a view of the data read from
        the file, so it costs nothing until used. Userdata is kept when
        transferring particles to files with the same layout (e.g. after
        transfer_metadata), and when merging, extracting or force-merging
        files with the Python API or pymcpltool, which give the same results
        as the C API and mcpltool.
      * The Python API can also write files with userdata: enable it with
        MCPLOutFile(..., userdata_layout = "temp:float32,tags:uint8[3]") or
        enable_userdata(layout), and give the userdata of the particles with
        the userdata argument of add_particles and add_particle, as a dict of
        field values (e.g. userdata = { 'temp' : temps, 'tags' : tags }) or a
        numpy structured array (like block.userdata). The userdata argument is
        required in files with userdata (except when transferring particles
        from files with the same layout), missing fields get zeros (so
        userdata = {} gives all bytes zero), and integer fields must be given
        integers within the range of their type. The files are identical to
        those written with the C API.
      * New examples of writing and reading userdata: examples/
        example_userdatamcpl.c (C) and examples/pyexample_userdatamcpl
        (Python).
      * Clearer error messages for invalid values of stat:sum entries (which
        must be non-negative or -1, and may only be padded with spaces).
      * Plotting backends in the Python API: the default backend can be set
        with mcpl.plotting.set_default_backend(...), and otherwise a backend is
        chosen automatically: for showing plots matplotlib (if it can show
        them here), else the unicode text backend (if the terminal can show
        its characters), else the ascii text backend, and for saving
        plots the best backend for the format given by the file extension.
        Custom backends can be added by subclassing mcpl.plotting.Backend
        (describing what they show plots on, their priority and their file
        formats) and calling mcpl.plotting.register_backend, and
        mcpl.plotting.formats lists the file formats which can be saved. Options for backends are named with
        the backend name as prefix (matplotlib_backend, unicode_width), and
        can be given to all plotting functions.
      * NaN and infinite values are no longer allowed in the particle data of
        MCPL files. Adding particles with such values (to fields which are
        stored) now gives an error, in the C API (mcpl_add_particle and
        mcpl_transfer_last_read_particle, and thus also mcpltool) and in the
        Python API, as do values too large to be stored in single precision.
        Existing files with such values can still be read, with a warning (once
        per file). In the Python API, the wavelength attribute of particles and
        blocks now gives an error for particles which are not neutrons or
        gammas (instead of NaN) or have zero kinetic energy, and expressions
        give an error if they produce NaN or infinite values.
      * The mcpl Python package is now split into several internal modules.
        Its public API is unchanged, and available after "import mcpl" as
        before. The mcpl.mcpl module is kept for backwards compatibility, but
        using names from it now gives a DeprecationWarning (e.g. use
        mcpl.MCPLFile instead of mcpl.mcpl.MCPLFile). Running a copy of the
        mcpl.py file on its own is no longer supported. Use the pymcpltool
        command or "python -m mcpl" instead.
      * Messages and warnings from the Python API (e.g. "MCPL WARNING: Input
        file appears to not have been closed properly") are still printed by
        default, but can now be sent to the logger "mcpl" of Python's logging
        module instead, by calling mcpl.use_logging(). pymcpltool always prints
        them.
      * New pymcpltool option --traceback, which shows the Python traceback of
        errors, for debugging.
      * MCPLParticle and MCPLParticleBlock in the Python API get a wavelength
        attribute, the (de Broglie) wavelength in Aa of neutrons and gammas
        (NaN for other particles), calculated with relativistic kinematics for
        neutrons. The new module mcpl.constants contains the physical
        constants used (CODATA 2022).
      * The Python API can now also write MCPL files, with the new MCPLOutFile
        class (GitHub issues #54 and #70). Like the MCPLFile reader, it is
        implemented in pure Python, using numpy to efficiently process blocks of
        particles, and it has the same capabilities as the C API for writing
        files. Header settings can be given as keyword arguments named like the
        MCPLFile attributes, or with methods named like the functions of the C
        API. The written files are identical to those written by the C library.
        Particles read with MCPLFile can be added directly, and are then
        transferred exactly. For neutrons and gammas, the wavelength can be
        given instead of the kinetic energy. The new gzip_file function
        compresses files like mcpl_gzip_file in the C API. See also the new
        examples/pyexample_writemcpl.
      * Particle blocks in the Python API can be indexed with a boolean mask,
        indices or a slice, which gives a new block with those particles (e.g.
        block[block.pdgcode == 2112]). Such blocks can be passed to
        MCPLOutFile.add_particles like any other block, also together with new
        values of some of the fields (e.g. weight = 2 * block.weight). The new
        fileidx attribute of blocks gives the positions of their particles in
        the file, and fileidx is also the new name of the file_index attribute
        of particles (file_index still works, but is deprecated).
      * The Python API also gets the other file-level functions of the C API:
        merge_files, forcemerge_files, merge_inplace, can_merge, repair,
        name_helper, create_outfile_mpi and merge_outfiles_mpi. They give the
        same results, printouts and errors as the C functions.
      * pymcpltool can now also do what mcpltool does: --merge (also with
        --inplace), --forcemerge, --extract (with -l, -s, -p and --no-comment)
        and --repair, with the same results and printouts as mcpltool.
      * When reading files in the old MCPL-2 format, directions are now unpacked
        with fma() (in C, and with the same calculation in Python), so the
        results are identical on all platforms. Previously they could differ in
        the last bit, depending on whether the compiler used fused multiply-add
        instructions.
      * Particles can now be selected and edited with expressions in the Python
        API, with the new ParticleFilter and ParticleEdit classes, e.g.
        ParticleFilter("pdgcode == neutron and wavelength > 1.8Aa") or
        ParticleEdit("rotate_z(90deg);z += 1cm"), which are applied to blocks
        of particles. Values with units must always be given with units (like
        25meV, 0.18nm or 1us, but a plain 0 is fine), and expressions with
        missing or inconsistent units are rejected. The wavelength can only be
        used for neutrons and gammas, which must be picked first (as in
        "pdgcode == neutron and wavelength > 1.8Aa"). Edits are executed in
        order, and can use variables (e.g. "tmp = x;x = y;y = tmp"),
        swap(a,b), and rotations. ParticleValue gives the values of an
        expression. See also the new examples pyexample_filtermcpl and
        pyexample_editmcpl.
      * MCPLFile and MCPLOutFile accept pick and edit arguments (expressions
        or ParticleFilter/ParticleEdit objects), which pick and edit the
        particles read from (or written to) the files, e.g.
        MCPLFile("in.mcpl",pick="pdgcode == neutron",edit="z += 1m"). Edited
        particles are still transferred exactly by MCPLOutFile.add_particles,
        except for the edited fields. dump_file, MCPLFile.dump_particles and
        collect_stats, histogram and histogram2d also accept a pick argument.
      * mcpltool can now pick and edit particles with expressions, with the
        new -p (--pick) and -E (--edit) options, e.g. mcpltool -e -p "pdgcode
        == neutron" -E "wavelength = 1.8Aa" FILE OUT.mcpl.gz. They can be used
        when showing particles, and with -S, -w, -H and -e, as can -l and -s,
        which choose the range of particles in the file before -p and -E are
        applied. Like with -l and -s, stat:sum entries are marked as not
        available (-1) when -E modifies weights, unless the new
        --keep-statsum option is used. The header comments describe the
        picks and edits, unless --no-comment is used. To pick particles of a
        type, use e.g. -p "pdgcode == neutron" (mcpltool-c still has
        -pPDGCODE).
      * New module mcpl.plotting with a backend-independent description of
        plots (Figure, Panel, and the items StepHist, Hist2D, Bars, Scatter3D
        and Text), which can be shown or saved with matplotlib, with the MCPL
        logo (with the show and save functions, and available_backends to list
        the available ones).
      * plot_stats is removed: it gives an error saying what to use instead
        (the viewer, mcpl.view, or mcpl.histogram), and mcpltool -S only
        prints the summary of the particles. The --pdf and --gui options are
        removed (use -w or -H instead).
      * New histogram API: histograms of values given by expressions, e.g.
        mcpl.histogram(FILE,"ekin/eV",lethargy=True).plot(), with options for
        picking particles, logarithmic axes, separate curves for each particle
        type (by default, see display_by) or file, 2D histograms
        (mcpl.histogram2d), and printing the contents as text.
        mcpl.plot_histograms shows or saves several histograms, also on top of
        each other in one plot with overlay=True (e.g. to compare
        simulations).
      * New mcpltool -H (--hist) option for histograms of expressions, with
        options NAME=VALUE separated by ";" (see mcpltool -H help), e.g.
        mcpltool -H "ekin;logx" -p "pdgcode == neutron" FILE, also for 2D
        histograms and bar charts, and several at once. Plots are shown in a
        window (matplotlib) or in the terminal (with -t), or saved with -o
        FILE. mcpl.histogram also accepts these strings.
      * mcpltool --extract no longer leaves a broken output file behind when
        given an invalid -p argument.
      * ssw2mcpl and phits2mcpl no longer leave an unfinished output file behind
        when the files given with -c or -s are found to be invalid.
      * ssw2mcpl and phits2mcpl no longer read past the end of the contents of
        the files given with -c or -s when checking them.
      * The ssw2mcpl, mcpl2ssw, phits2mcpl and mcpl2phits commands from the
        mcpl-extra Python package no longer fail when mcpl-config is not in the
        PATH (e.g. when running them from a virtual environment which is not
        activated). If the mcpl-core package does not provide the _mcpl_core
        module (as on conda-forge), mcpl-config is still used.
      * Avoid misaligned memory access (undefined behaviour) when reading SSW
        and PHITS files in mcpl-extra.
      * The comment added by mcpltool --extract to the header of the new file
        now describes how particles were selected (e.g. "mcpltool: extracted
        particles with -l100 -p2112"), instead of giving the number of particles
        in the input file. Files extracted in the same way from different files
        can thus be merged. The new --no-comment option disables the comment.
      * mcpltool --extract -p now also accepts the names neutron, antineutron,
        proton, antiproton, electron, positron (or antielectron), muon,
        antimuon, and gamma (or photon) instead of PDG codes, e.g. -pneutron.
      * Fix mcpl_transfer_last_read_particle for transfers from files with
        double precision to files with single precision, which wrote wrong
        kinetic energies and directions. The transfers done by mcpltool were not
        affected.
      * pymcpltool --stats (and dump_stats in the Python API) now also shows
        any stat:sum entries of the file (GitHub issue #94).
      * mcpltool --extract now only gzips the output file if its name ends with
        .mcpl.gz, like --merge does (GitHub issue #21). Previously it always
        gzipped, and an output name ending with .mcpl.gz resulted in a file
        named .mcpl.gz.mcpl.gz. Both now also refuse to overwrite an existing
        file when the .mcpl extension is left out of the output name.
      * Fix typo in error message of mcpl_hdr_add_stat_sum.
      * mcpl_hdr_add_comment now refuses to add a stat:sum: comment with the
        same key as an existing one, since such files can not be read.
      * Drop support for Python 3.8.
      * mcpltool --repair (and mcpl_repair) now also repairs files truncated
        right after the header, and removes any partially written particle at
        the end of the file.
      * Opening a file which was not closed properly now also gives a warning
        if it only contains part of the first particle.
      * ...

